  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how two stem-cell lineages generate major blood and skeletal tissue cell types. At the top, a hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) is shown as the common source of circulating blood elements, branching into erythrocytes, platelets, and leukocytes. This conveys the familiar blood-forming pathway in which one progenitor gives rise to red cells, clotting fragments, and white immune cells.\n\nBelow that, mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) are divided into two related sources, labeled extraosseous MSC and bone marrow MSC. From this mesenchymal pool, arrows lead to adipocytes, osteoblasts, and chondrocytes, indicating differentiation into fat, bone-forming, and cartilage-forming cells. The central osteoblast is emphasized, with arrows suggesting a close relationship both with bone marrow MSCs and with another cell type labeled OCR at the bottom. OCR appears able to contribute to both osteoblast and chondrocyte lineages.\n\nOverall, the diagram contrasts hematopoietic differentiation with mesenchymal differentiation and highlights osteoblasts as a central product of bone marrow-derived mesenchymal cells, while also linking cartilage and bone lineages through a shared progenitor-like route.",
